"English divides the year into four seasons, and most health advice that mentions seasons at all stays just as broad — \"eat warm foods in winter,\" \"stay hydrated in summer.\" Chinese medicine divides the year into twenty-four solar terms instead, each lasting roughly fifteen days and marking the sun's precise position along its yearly path. The difference isn't decorative. A piece of advice meant to cover a three-month season is almost always too vague to be useful; a piece of advice tied to a fifteen-day window can be specific enough to actually follow. Each solar term carries its own name and its own expected pattern of change — some mark shifts in temperature, like \"slight cold\" and \"great cold\" in the depths of winter; some mark shifts in the visible world, like \"insects awaken\" in early spring, when hibernating creatures are said to stir; some mark agricultural turning points, like \"grain in ear,\" once used to time the harvest. The names function like fifteen-day chapter headings for the year, each one flagging a specific transition worth adjusting for. For someone learning to apply Chinese medicine's ideas about qi, yin-yang, and the organs to daily life, the twenty-four solar terms are the actual calendar the whole system runs on. Advice like \"protect the liver's smooth qi in spring\" only becomes useful once it's anchored to a specific window of weeks, rather than left floating across an entire season three months long.": "d3f8f185d5ba",
